# What each indirect call site reaches, per track, for tools/stack_depth.py.
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, and the opposite of tools/stream_seam.list in one
# important way: that file is generated from the source and a test holds
# it equal to a fresh extraction. This one cannot be. Every line here is
# a CLAIM about what a function pointer can point at, made by a person,
# and no extraction can confirm it - which is exactly why it is tracked
# rather than retyped into a shell each time. A claim in shell history
# is a claim nobody can review.
#
# Format, whitespace separated, `#` to end of line is a comment:
#
#     <track>  indirect  <location substring>  <spec>
#     <track>  leaf      <symbol>              <bytes>
#
# `indirect` specs, in descending order of how much they are worth:
#
#   <symbol>   a const dispatch table. EXACT - the tool reads the array
#              out of the linked ELF, masks the Thumb bit and maps the
#              addresses back through the symbol table, so the target
#              set is re-derived from every build and cannot drift from
#              the source the way an annotation can.
#   vtable:C   a C++ virtual call, resolved from class C's vtable, which
#              is a const array in the image and is read the same way.
#              EXACT, and the claim in it is that C is the call site's
#              static type.
#   vtable     every vtable in the image. Sound for a virtual call and
#              tight, but a union over classes, so the answer is
#              labelled `upper bound`.
#   .../N/A    either vtable spec with a method filter appended -
#              `vtable/write/1`, `vtable:Serial_/accept/0` - keeping
#              only the slots that hold that name at that arity. The
#              one spec here that REMOVES chains, because a vtable read
#              cannot see which overload a call site named and reaches
#              every slot without it. So it needs the site's own source
#              line as its evidence. Its guard is that a filter
#              matching NO slot is refused rather than resolved to
#              nothing: a misspelt method and a method with no override
#              are the same empty set from in here, and one of them is
#              a silent under-report.
#   target:S   one named function. A claim, but one that ADDS a chain,
#              so it cannot under-report.
#   noreturn   the site does not come back, so it contributes no chain.
#   none       no target is ever registered. The weakest of these and
#              the only one resting on nothing but the writer: it
#              needs a reason on the line, and the reason needs to name
#              what would falsify it.
#
# A site with no line here is REFUSED, not assumed. That is the point of
# the file: adding a function pointer to this firmware breaks the build
# report until somebody says what it reaches.

# --- Track B -----------------------------------------------------------
# The console's 50-letter dispatch. `console_bindings` is `const`, so the
# table is in .rodata and resolves exactly: 50 entries, 50 targets, 0
# unknown.
b  indirect  console.c:260        console_bindings
# `f` proves the fault handler by branching to an address that is not
# code. It faults rather than returning, so there is no chain past it.
b  indirect  console_cmds.c:44    noreturn

# --- Track C -----------------------------------------------------------
# Same two, from the same shared source.
c  indirect  console.c:260        console_bindings
c  indirect  console_cmds.c:44    noreturn
# FreeRTOS's software-timer dispatch, three sites in timers.c. This build
# sets configUSE_TIMERS 1 - so the timer task exists and the callback
# call sites are compiled - but nothing anywhere calls xTimerCreate, so
# no callback is ever registered and the list the sites walk is empty.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS: the first xTimerCreate() in apps/rtos_bringup.
# At that point this line is wrong and the honest replacement is a table
# spec naming wherever the callbacks come from, or nothing at all - and
# the tool refusing is the correct outcome until one exists.
c  indirect  timers.c             none

# --- Track A -----------------------------------------------------------
# The console, same shared source and same two sites as B and C.
a  indirect  console.c:260        console_bindings
a  indirect  console_cmds.c:44    noreturn

# The core's Print/Stream hierarchy, ONE LINE PER CALL SITE, and the
# method each one names is the whole reason these are not four lines.
#
# A vtable read cannot see which overload the call site resolved to:
# the .ci records the site's file, line and column and nothing about
# the callee, so an unfiltered read reaches every slot in the table.
# Print::write(buf, len) loops calling write(c), and slot 1 of every
# concrete table is the INHERITED Print::write(buf, len) - so the
# method appears to call itself, and the walk reported recursion in
# firmware that has none. `/NAME/ARITY` is the source's own overload
# resolution written down; arity rather than types because the call
# graph spells a type `const uint8_t*` and nm -C spells it
# `unsigned char const*`.
#
# Print and Stream are abstract bases with no instances, so no vtable
# exists for either and a class-named spec cannot resolve. A virtual
# call through them lands in a concrete subclass, and the union of the
# image's vtables is exactly that set - sound, and the answer is
# labelled `upper bound` because it is a union over classes.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY ANY OF THESE: the call site changing which
# overload it names. The tool refuses on a filter that matches no slot,
# so a renamed or re-signatured method is a refusal and not a silent
# zero - but a site edited to call a DIFFERENT existing overload would
# resolve to the wrong slot and is what a reader should check these
# lines against.
#
# Print::write(const uint8_t*, size_t):38 -> write(c), one argument.
a  indirect  Print.cpp:38         vtable/write/1
# Print::print(char):61, print(long, int):82 and print(unsigned long,
# int):97 -> write(n), one argument by integral conversion; the other
# three write() overloads take a pointer and are not viable.
a  indirect  Print.cpp:61         vtable/write/1
a  indirect  Print.cpp:82         vtable/write/1
a  indirect  Print.cpp:97         vtable/write/1
# Print::write(const char*):51 and write(const char*, size_t):55, both
# inline in Print.h -> write(buf, size), two arguments.
a  indirect  Print.h:51           vtable/write/2
a  indirect  Print.h:55           vtable/write/2
# Print::print(const Printable&):115 -> x.printTo(*this). No vtable in
# the image holds printTo, because nothing here derives from Printable:
# the core's only Printable subclass is IPAddress and --gc-sections
# discarded every one of its methods. So the site has no reachable
# target. Spelled `none` rather than a printTo filter deliberately - a
# filter that matches nothing is refused, and it should be, because a
# misspelt method looks identical to this from inside the tool. The
# claim belongs in a line a reader can challenge.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS: a `vtable for X` in the image whose slots
# include printTo - equivalently, the first Printable subclass Track A
# instantiates.
a  indirect  Print.cpp:115        none
# UARTClass::end():87 -> flush(). `this` is a UARTClass, which names
# the table; USARTClass derives from it and does not override flush, so
# its slot holds UARTClass::flush and the narrower read loses nothing.
a  indirect  UARTClass.cpp:87     vtable:UARTClass/flush/0

# Serial_::read():252 calling accept(), and Serial_::write(uint8_t):291
# calling write(buf, len). Both are `virtual` in USB/USBAPI.h and
# dispatched through `this`, whose static type is Serial_, so the class
# names the table; nothing derives from Serial_.
#
# THE CLASS IS LOAD-BEARING AT :291 and the method filter alone does
# not close it. Bare `vtable/write/2` reaches Print::write(buf, len) as
# well as Serial_'s override, and Print::write(buf, len) calls
# write(c), which reaches back to Serial_::write(uint8_t) - a cycle
# through two tables belonging to two different objects. Measured:
# every site bare refuses with
# `Print::write(const uint8_t*, size_t) -> Serial_::write(uint8_t) ->
# Print::write(const uint8_t*, size_t)`.
a  indirect  CDC.cpp:252          vtable:Serial_/accept/0
a  indirect  CDC.cpp:291          vtable:Serial_/write/2

# Stream.cpp had a line here and has no site: Stream::timedRead() and
# timedPeek() call read() and peek() virtually, and --gc-sections
# discarded both along with every parsing helper that reaches them.
# Removed rather than kept as a dormant claim - the tool refuses on an
# undeclared site, so the day one of them is linked in, the report
# stops rather than resolving on a line nobody re-read.

# PluggableUSB's four virtuals over the plugged-module list. rootNode is
# written only by PluggableUSB_::plug(), and this firmware calls it
# exactly once - sketches/bringup/ctlusb.cpp, with a CtlUSB. The core's
# other plug() is in libraries/HID, which Track A does not compile.
a  indirect  PluggableUSB.cpp     vtable:CtlUSB

# attachInterrupt's four PIO callback tables. PIOA-D_Handler are in the
# image so the sites can execute, but attachInterrupt is NOT - nothing
# under sketches/, lib/, drivers/ or apps/ calls it and --gc-sections
# took it - and it is the only writer of the four `static interruptCB
# [32]` arrays besides __initialize, which NULLs them.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS: the first attachInterrupt() in Track A. The
# replacement is not a table spec, because callbacksPioA is .bss and
# reading it out of the ELF would report zero targets while claiming to
# be exact.
a  indirect  WInterrupts.c        none

# UOTGHS's dispatch to the stack's ISR. `gpf_isr` is .bss, set by
# UDD_SetStack(&USB_ISR) from USBDevice_'s constructor on the
# static-init path, so `none` is provably FALSE and a table read finds a
# NOBITS section. Named directly: the claim adds a chain rather than
# removing one, so it cannot under-report, and this is the USB interrupt
# stack - the site where an under-report would matter most.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS: a second UDD_SetStack() call with a different
# handler.
a  indirect  uotghs.c:39          target:USB_ISR

# libsam's EMAC and EFC callbacks need no line: neither emac_handler nor
# efc_perform_command is in the linked image, so the tool drops their
# sites with the rest of what --gc-sections discarded. Recorded here
# because their absence from this file is a decision, not an oversight.

# --- How Track A got a bound, and the three wrong reasons on the way ---
#
# It has one now: 880 B on Reset_Handler, upper bound, linux-x1,
# Debian arm-gcc 14.2.1. Every line above this point is what it took,
# and the three explanations below are kept because each one was
# asserted and then refuted by a short experiment - the first of them
# from inside this file, where a guess reads as established.
#
# THE FIRST WAS WRONG. It said the
# vendored core is compiled `-w` so -fcallgraph-info does not reach it.
# `-w` inhibits warnings and has nothing to do with this flag; measured,
# the flag reaches the core fine - 79 .ci files, 58 of them from
# track_a_core. That claim was asserted rather than tested, which is the
# thing CLAUDE.md says to do to "the core will not let us" before
# believing it.
#
# Two real reasons, and only the second is Track A's own.
#
# C++ NAME MANGLING. Track A is C++, so GCC writes the DEMANGLED
# signature into the .ci label - `void watchdogSetup()`,
# `RingBuffer::RingBuffer()` - while nm and `objdump --disassemble=`
# want the mangled symbol. Every leaf lookup misses and the tool
# refuses. 12 of the 33 unresolved leaves are only this, and are
# defined in the image. It is the same defect issue #45 hit doing
# dead-function analysis on this track, recurring in a new tool: a
# plain-name match against C++ is wrong in both directions.
#
# NEWLIB, which is the substantive one. Track A links the C library and
# calls into it - malloc, free, atof, memcmp, memmove, isspace - and
# none of those is compiled here, so 21 leaves have no frame anywhere in
# the tree. Track B avoids the C library on the working path and has
# six. That is a real difference between the tracks rather than a
# tooling gap, and closing it means declaring those frames with `leaf`
# lines, each carrying how it was measured.
#
# AND THAT SECOND REASON WAS ALSO WRONG, measured a day later. Neither
# track uses newlib's heap or stdio. Both link the same slice - memcpy,
# memset, strlen, the __aeabi helpers - and `malloc`, `free`, `atof`,
# `String::String` are in NEITHER image: the core's source references
# them, and --gc-sections discards every one. 25 of the 33 leaves were
# functions the linker had already deleted.
#
# The one real retained difference is that Track A pulls 18
# double-precision soft-float helpers (__aeabi_dadd, __aeabi_ddiv, ...)
# that Track B does not, which is the core's double-based API on a part
# with no FPU.
#
# What actually stops Track A, third time of asking and this one
# measured end to end: THE ARDUINO CORE IS BUILT ON C++ VIRTUAL DISPATCH
# AND RUNTIME CALLBACK REGISTRATION. 29 indirect call sites in vendored
# code - Print, Stream, UARTClass, PluggableUSB, CDC, attachInterrupt,
# and libsam's emac/efc/uotghs - against Track B's 2 and Track C's 5. A
# virtual call's target set is "every override of this method", which is
# the one pattern a static call graph cannot follow without
# devirtualisation.
#
# So Track A needs a declaration per virtual call site, most of them
# about code this project did not write. That much was right. What was
# wrong was the last sentence, which said the date was further out:
# nineteen lines closed it, and the missing piece was one rung on the
# ladder rather than a new analysis.
#
# THE FOURTH WRONG REASON WAS THE COST OF THE FIX, and it is the one
# worth carrying. Closing the cycle looked as though it needed
# call-site arity from somewhere the compiler does not record it, or an
# LTO build with -fwhole-program-vtables. Neither was needed. The
# arity is in the SOURCE, one line above the site the .ci already
# names, and writing it down is the same act as every other line in
# this file: a claim, with its evidence, that the tool then re-derives
# from the image on every build. Asking the compiler for what a person
# can read off the call site was three of the four wrong answers here
# in a different costume.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, so that nobody reads more into 880 B than is
# there. The bound is an `upper bound`, not exact, and the reason is
# the union over classes: a virtual call inside an abstract base can
# land in any concrete subclass, so `vtable/write/1` takes the write
# slot of every table in the image. Narrowing each site to the
# receivers it can really have was tried and moved no root's figure at
# all - 880 either way - so it buys the word `exact` and nothing else,
# at the price of ten more claims that can under-report. Measured
# rather than argued, and declined on the measurement.
